2021 could be on pace to be america's  deadliest year of gun violence in the  last two decades  yesterday president biden announced a  new strategy to combat it  and includes a zero tolerance policy for  gun dealers who break federal law  deploying strike teams to cities to  crack down on illegal gun trafficking  and encouraging communities to use  federal pandemic relief funds for  policing and crime prevention  our lead national correspondent david  begno is in new york city with a look at  the rise  of violence that led to the new  initiative david good morning  good morning we wanted to know why so we  went to princeton university to speak  with a sociologist who studies crime for  a living  and he believes there are three shocks  that have contributed to the rise in  crime number one  the shutdown of core institutions during  the pandemic  second a record number of gun sales and  third protests during the pandemic in  the aftermath of those protests  you'll hear from that sociologist in a  moment but this morning we're focusing  on the specific number of murders that  are on the rise  take chicago last weekend police say  during the puerto rican day festivities  surveillance video captured giovanni  orzwaga and yasmine perez being ambushed  after a minor traffic accident  according to authorities yasmine was  pulled from the car beaten  and shot when giovanni tried to save her  he was gunned down almost  execution style both of them were killed  it's hard to believe that he's gone a  young guy is gone  just because of an accident in new york  city two children narrowly avoided being  shot as a gunman  shot at a man on a busy city street  and in austin texas this month gunfire  broke out early one morning in the  city's nightlife district  one person was killed 13 more were  injured  nearly 10 000 people have been killed  this year alone  from gun violence and it's not letting  up in a study of 24 major cities  homicides increased by 24  in the first quarter of 2021 compared to  the same time period in 2020  and by 49 from that period in 2019  so far this year homicides are up nearly  14  in new york city 22 in los angeles  and by 13 in washington d.c compared to  last year  the frustrating thing is no one has to  has  identified the the magic solution  stephen adler is the mayor of austin  texas and he is hoping to reverse the  trends  the city is on track to greatly surpass  its  own homicide record which was set just  last year  we have too many illegal weapons that  are falling into the wrong hands  we have to try to find those illegal  weapons and take them off the street  for patrick sharkey a sociologist at  princeton university  the answer goes well beyond illegal  firearms and law enforcement  in the u.s we have this knee-jerk  reaction where we think only about the  police and the prison system  as the responsibility as a sociologist  who studies gun violence in america  do you believe that abolishing the  police is a good idea  no i don't think it's a good idea we  have  300 400 million guns on the street so we  don't have another set of institutions  that can respond to gun violence like  the police can  our primary focus should be investing in  these other institutions  that can deal with addiction mental  illness provide after school programs  here's some perspective there's one  analysis that took a look at about 20  big cities around the country and found  that roughly one percent of the calls  police officers are responding on  involve calls of violence where a gun or  a knife is involved just one percent  so the sociologist you just heard from  is a proponent of let's save the police  for those calls  but let's send in better trained people  to deal with a large majority  of everything else david bagno for us in  new york  thank you
 

